#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Validate a MAVLink stream with pymavlink — independently of our own encoder.

Our JUnit tests encode with `io.dronefleet.mavlink` and assert with the same library, so they
prove our arithmetic matches our intent and nothing more. mavverify is the second opinion: it
decodes with **pymavlink** and checks the wire (framing, CRCs, sequence continuity, MAVLink 2,
sysid/compid), the measured per-message rates, physical plausibility, and unit agreement
*between our own messages* — a 1000x (mm vs m) or 100x (cdeg vs deg) disagreement no test that
restates the implementation can notice. With `--expect-*` it adds the one check no software
test can substitute for: the stream against the aircraft's real measured position.

    tools/mavverify capture.jsonl                      # a mavcapture-1 file
    tools/mavverify --port 14550 --seconds 20          # live, sitting where QGC sits
    tools/mavcapture --from-pcap tap.pcap --out tap.jsonl && tools/mavverify tap.jsonl
                                                       # a read-only tcpdump tap at the relay,
                                                       # the way to verify without disturbing QGC
    tools/mavverify capture.jsonl --require-set none   # wire+units only, no schedule
    tools/mavverify capture.jsonl --expect-site ground-probe        # ground truth
    tools/mavverify capture.jsonl --expect-lat 37.99386 --expect-lon 23.72533 \
                                 --expect-amsl 103.2 --tolerance-m 50
    tools/mavverify --list-checks                      # every check + its provenance
    tools/mavverify --show-limits                      # every declared limit + source

Exits non-zero on any FAIL (and on WARN with --strict), so it works as a pre-flight gate. Run
inside the nix dev shell — it provides pymavlink.

Full rationale — the four layers, what each cannot catch, and the pre-flight procedure:
`docs/verification.md`.
"""

